> How to use CSS to change the color of jokes to red?
[0m```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Random Joke Website</title>
  <style>
    #jokeDisplay {
      color: red;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Tell Me a Joke!</h1>
  <button id="jokeButton">Get a Joke</button>
  <p id="jokeDisplay"></p>

  <script>
    // JavaScript code from previous example (unchanged)
  </script>
</body>
</html>

```

**Explanation:**

1. **Inline Style (Not Recommended):**  You could add the styling directly within the `<p>` tag:
   ```html
   <p id="jokeDisplay" style="color: red;"> </p>
   ```

2. **Internal Style Sheet (Within `<head>`):** We've used this method here.
   - Create a `<style>` tag inside the `<head>` section.
   - Within the `<style>` tags, write CSS rules.
   - To target the paragraph with the `id` "jokeDisplay", we use `#jokeDisplay { color: red; }`.

**How it Works:**

- The CSS rule `#jokeDisplay { color: red; }` says: "For any element with the ID `jokeDisplay`, set its text color to red."

- When the browser parses the HTML, it applies the CSS rules to the corresponding elements. So, the text inside the `<p>` with `id="jokeDisplay"` will now be displayed in red.



